{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And for totafot between your eyes</b> — This word has no known cognate family, but the masters of the language would seek to derive it from the root of \"and drip toward the south\" (Ezekiel 21:2), \"and upon them my speech drips\" (Job 29:22), a meaning borrowed from \"and the mountains shall drip sweet wine\" (Amos 9:13) — He would say that you shall make the Exodus from Egypt a sign upon your arm and a discourse between your eyes, distilling like dew upon those who hear it. But our Sages call the object placed on the head \"totafot,\" as they said (Shabbat 57b): neither with a totafet nor with a sanbutin. And Rabbi Abbahu said there: What is a totafet? A band that encircles from ear to ear. And they are the masters of the language who speak in it and know it, and from them it is proper to receive it. And Scripture says <b>totafot</b> (plural) and does not say \"totafet\" (singular), because they are multiple compartments, as we have received their form from the holy fathers, which the prophets and the early ones practiced thus until Moses our master.\n\nNow the root of this commandment is that we place the text of the Exodus from Egypt upon the hand and upon the head, corresponding to the heart and the brain, which are the seats of thought. And so the passage of \"Sanctify\" (קַדֵּשׁ) and \"And it shall be when He brings you\" (וְהָיָה כִּי יְבִיאֲךָ) is written in the totafot, because of this commandment in which we are commanded to make the Exodus from Egypt totafot between our eyes. And in the passage of \"Hear\" (שְׁמַע) and \"And it shall be if you hear\" (וְהָיָה אִם שָׁמוֹעַ), we are commanded to make the commandments also into totafot, as it is written (Deuteronomy 6:6): \"And these words that I command you today shall be upon your heart, and they shall be as totafot between your eyes.\" Therefore we also write both of those passages for totafot, for they are the commandment of unity and the remembrance of all the commandments and their punishment and their reward, and the entire root is faith. And regarding the hand-tefillin it says: \"And it shall be for you a sign upon your hand,\" and they interpreted this (Menahot 36b) to mean the left hand, toward which the heart inclines.\n\nAnd by way of truth, what Scripture says — \"because of this, that YHWH did for me\" — is like \"This is my El and I will glorify Him\" (Exodus 15:2): He would say that for the sake of His name and His glory He acted with us and brought us out of Egypt, and this shall be for you a sign upon the arm of your strength, in the sense of \"for You are the splendor of their strength\" (Psalms 89:18). And behold, the sign is like the sign of circumcision and the sign of Sabbath. And because all is included in the sign upon the hand, our fathers transmitted from Moses, from the mouth of the Almighty, that it shall be one compartment, in the manner that Scripture says: \"My sister, my bride\" (Song of Songs 5:1) — because she is unified and comprised of thirty-two paths. And it is written: \"His left hand is under my head\" (Song of Songs 2:6).\n\nAnd He said <b>and for a remembrance between your eyes</b> — that they shall be placed in the place of memory between the eyes, which is the forepart of the brain, and it is the beginning of memory and the locus of forms after they separate from before it. And they encompass the entire head with their straps, and the knot that is at the rear of the brain preserves the memory. And the expression \"between your eyes\" means that they shall be at the middle of the head, not on one side — or that there are the roots of the eyes, and from there vision proceeds. Similarly, \"You shall not make a baldness between your eyes for the dead\" — and to explain this He returned and said <b>and for totafot</b>, to make clear that the commandment is not between the eyes below, but rather on the crown of the head they are placed there as totafot. And He used a plural form, because they are multiple compartments, as we have received.\n\nNow I shall declare to you a general principle regarding the reason for many commandments. From the time that idolatry arose in the world, from the days of Enosh, the doctrines concerning faith began to go astray. Some deny the root principle and say that the world is eternal (קַדְמוֹן) — they denied YHWH and said, \"It is not He\" (Jeremiah 5:12). Some deny His particular knowledge and say, \"How does El know? Is there knowledge in the Most High?\" (Psalms 73:11). And some acknowledge the knowledge but deny providence, and they made man like the fish of the sea — that El does not attend to them, and there is no punishment or reward; they say, \"YHWH has forsaken the land.\" But when the Elohim desires a community or an individual and performs with them a wonder involving a change in the order and nature of the world, the nullification of all these doctrines becomes clear to all. For the extraordinary wonder demonstrates that the world has a deity who created it anew (מְחַדְּשׁוֹ), and who knows, and attends, and is able. And when that wonder is declared beforehand from the mouth of a prophet, the truth of prophecy is further demonstrated through it — that the Elohim speaks with man and reveals His secret to His servants the prophets. And together with this, the entire Torah is established.\n\nTherefore Scripture says concerning the wonders: \"That you may know that I am YHWH in the midst of the earth\" (Exodus 8:18) — to demonstrate providence, that He has not abandoned it to chance, as in their opinion. And He said (Exodus 9:29): \"That you may know that the earth is YHWH's\" — to demonstrate creation anew (חִדּוּשׁ), for it is His, since He created it from nothing. And He said (Exodus 9:14): \"So that you may know that there is none like Me in all the earth\" — to demonstrate omnipotence, that He rules over all and there is no hindrance in His hand — for in all these things the Egyptians were either denying or doubting. If so, the great signs and wonders are faithful witnesses to the faith of the Creator and to the entire Torah.\n\nAnd because the Holy One, blessed be He, does not perform a sign or wonder in every generation before the eyes of every wicked person or denier, He commands us that we make a constant remembrance and sign of what our eyes have seen, and that we transmit the matter to our children, and their children to their children, and their children to the last generation. And He was exceedingly stringent in this matter, as He imposed excision (כָּרֵת) for eating leaven (Exodus 12:15) and for neglecting the Paschal offering (Numbers 9:13), and required that we write everything that we saw in the signs and wonders upon our hands and between our eyes, and further write it upon the doorposts of our houses in the mezuzot, and that we mention this with our mouths morning and evening — as they said (Berakhot 21a): \"Truth and Firmness\" — that the recitation of the morning Shema is ordained from the fact that it is written (Deuteronomy 16:3): \"That you may remember the day of your departure from the land of Egypt all the days of your life,\" and that we make a sukkah every year.\n\nAnd likewise, all commandments similar to these — many commandments that are a remembrance of the Exodus from Egypt — all are so that they may be for us, in every generation, a testimony through wonders that shall not be forgotten, and there shall be no opening of the mouth for a denier to contradict the faith in Elohim. For one who acquires a mezuzah for a single zuz and affixes it to his door and intends in its meaning has already acknowledged the creation of the world and the knowledge of the Creator and His providence, and also in prophecy, and has believed in all the facets of the Torah — besides acknowledging that the Creator's kindness is very great toward those who do His will, for He brought us out from that servitude to freedom and to great glory, for the merit of their fathers who desired the fear of His name.\n\nTherefore they said (Avot 2:1): Be as careful with a light commandment as with a weighty one, for all of them are exceedingly precious and beloved, since at every moment a person acknowledges through them his Elohim. And the intent of all the commandments is that we believe in our Elohim and acknowledge Him that He created us — and this is the intent of creation, for we have no other reason for the first creation, and the Supernal El desires nothing from those below except that a person know and acknowledge his Elohim who created him. And the intent of raising the voice in prayer, and the intent of synagogues, and the merit of the prayer of the many — this is that human beings have a place where they gather and acknowledge the El who created them and brought them into being, and they make this known and say before Him: \"We are Your creatures.\" And this is their intent in what the Sages, of blessed memory, said (Jerusalem Talmud, Ta'anit 2:1): \"And they cried unto the Elohim with might\" (Jonah 3:8) — from here you learn that prayer requires a voice; the bold one defeats the clothed one.\n\nAnd from the great and publicized miracles, a person comes to acknowledge the hidden miracles, which are the foundation of the entire Torah. For a person has no share in the Torah of Moses our master until we believe that all our words and all our events are all miracles — they have no nature and no worldly order, whether in the case of the multitudes or the individual. Rather, if one performs the commandments, its reward will make him prosper; and if he transgresses them, its punishment will cut him off — all by decree of the Most High, as I have already mentioned (Genesis 17:1, and above Exodus 6:2). And the hidden miracles will be publicized through the public, as will be seen in the admonitions of the Torah regarding the blessings and the curses, as Scripture says (Deuteronomy 29:23–24): \"And all the nations shall say: For what did YHWH do thus to this land? And they shall say: Because they forsook the covenant of YHWH, the Elohim of their fathers\" — that the matter will become publicized to all the nations that it is from YHWH, in their punishment. And concerning observance He said: \"And all the peoples of the earth shall see that the name of YHWH is called upon you, and they shall fear you.\" And I shall explain this further, with the help of the Name (Leviticus 26:11)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :register {:flags {0 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {0 "The phrase חַצִּיפָא נָצַח לְבִישָׁא is an Aramaic idiom cited from the Jerusalem Talmud; the Arukh (s.v. חצף) glosses it in ways that vary between witnesses. Translated here hyper-literally as 'the bold one defeats the clothed one,' following Ramban's explicit citation of the Arukh, without resolving the idiom's precise meaning."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:08:21.519290850Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}